  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1220 AM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nSimilar setup Wednesday, but with less shear and a lower risk for\nsevere storms. The Southwest Mountains will continue to be \nfavored for more robust convection due to higher PWATs, with the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall given slow storm motion. A \nmoderate to major heat risk is forecast Wednesday across the \nNorthwest Plateau and another advisory may be required. Smoke \nimpacts from UT/CO wildfires will likely persist, especially \nacross northwest and north central NM. The Monsoon high is then \nforecast to move northwest to over NV/UT through the end of the \nweek, but will extend a ridge axis into northwest NM and keep \nconditions hot and dry there. High temperatures in Farmington are \nforecast to reach 100 degrees or greater through at least Friday,\nmaking this the longest streak of 100+ degree days in Farmington\never in the month of August (previously 3 days). Elsewhere, above\naverage temperatures and below average convective coverage for \nearly August are forecast to persist.",
